---
title: My Favorite Books pt. 2
blurb: Three more I keep recommending, this time on sitting still, writing clearly, and the rules nobody mentions.
date: 2026-10-13
tags: [misc]
---

The first one of these went over well enough that I kept a list going. Here are
three more, and none of them are about software.

## The Three Pillars of Zen

<p class="book-author">Roshi Philip Kapleau</p>

<img
	src="/books/the-three-pillars-of-zen.webp"
	alt="The Three Pillars of Zen, 35th anniversary edition cover"
	width="440"
	height="679"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Kapleau was an American court reporter who covered the war crimes trials, went to
Japan, and stayed thirteen years to train. The book he came back with is not a book
about Zen so much as a set of primary documents. You get a teacher's lecture series
on the actual mechanics of sitting, transcripts of private interviews between the
teacher and his students, and then a long section of first-person accounts from
people describing what happened to them. The interview transcripts are the part I
keep returning to. A student arrives with a tidy intellectual account of what they
have understood, and the teacher simply declines to accept it, over and over, until
the student stops performing. It is the least mystical book about enlightenment I
have read, which is why it works on me.

Takeaways:

- The posture instructions are not a warmup for the real thing, they are the thing.
  A surprising amount of practice turns out to be logistics you were hoping to skip.
- A clean explanation of an experience is not the experience. I notice this in code
  reviews now, when someone describes a system beautifully and has clearly not run
  it.

## Writing with Style

<p class="book-author">John R. Trimble</p>

<img
	src="/books/writing-with-style.webp"
	alt="Writing with Style: Conversations on the Art of Writing cover"
	width="440"
	height="646"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Short, blunt, and the only style guide I have ever finished. Trimble's premise is
that most bad writing is not a grammar problem, it is a courtesy problem. The writer
is busy expressing himself and has forgotten that a stranger has to follow this
sentence by sentence with no idea where it is going. So the book is mostly about the
reader: how to open without clearing your throat for a paragraph, how to carry
someone across a transition, where to put the thing you most want them to remember.
He also spends a cheerful chapter killing the rules you were taught and never
questioned, like the one about prepositions at the end of a sentence. The advice
holds up for commit messages and design docs, which is most of what I write.

Takeaways:

- Write the draft to find out what you think, then rewrite it for the person who has
  to read it. Those are two different jobs and doing them at once produces neither.
- If a sentence is hard to follow, that is your problem to fix, not the reader's
  problem to push through.

## 23 Things They Don't Tell You About Capitalism

<p class="book-author">Ha-Joon Chang</p>

<img
	src="/books/23-things-about-capitalism.webp"
	alt="23 Things They Don't Tell You About Capitalism cover"
	width="440"
	height="678"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Twenty-three short chapters, each one opening with what you have been told and then
taking it apart. Chang is a Cambridge economist and not remotely anti-capitalist,
which is what makes the book land. He is arguing that the version of capitalism most
people treat as the natural state of things is one design among several, assembled
out of choices that were fought over and then forgotten. The opening chapter claims
there is no such thing as a free market, and the argument is simply that every market
already runs on rules you stopped noticing, like who is allowed to work and what may
be sold at all. My favorite chapter argues the washing machine changed the world more
than the internet did, and he is annoyingly convincing about it. You do not have to
accept all twenty-three to come out of it reading economic claims more carefully.

Takeaways:

- When something looks like the absence of rules, it usually means the rules are old
  enough that everyone stopped seeing them. Same is true of a codebase.
- The technology that feels revolutionary and the technology that actually
  rearranged how people live are rarely the same one.

That is part two. There will probably be a part three eventually.
